On Tue, Sep 30, 2014 at 6:05 PM, Marcus Brubaker <marcus.a...@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Tue, Sep 30, 2014 at 11:39 AM, Christopher Gandrud <christoph...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Hi Marcus
> >
> > Thanks for the insight.
> >
> > I actually do want to create a country (j) specific sigma_alpha. So sigma_alpha should be the same for all all alpha[t+1] for each j. This is for a couple of reasons, the simple on being that not doing so seems to lead the alpha parameters to grow dramatically.
> >
> > I would like to vectorize this though so that I only need to call sigma_alpha ~ cauchy(0,0.25) and it will have J values.
>
> I just checked and the Cauchy distribution should be vectorized so if you had:
>
> parameters {
>   ...
>   real<lower=0> sigma_alpha[J];
>   ...
> }
>
> Then:
>
> model {
>   ...
>   sigma_alpha ~ cauchy(0,0.25);
>   ...
> }
>
> should work just fine.  If it doesn't, what error does it produce?
>
> > Thanks for the block suggestion. Any chance you could point me to an example?
>
> For a single sigma_alpha:
>
> block(alpha,1,2,J,T-1) ~ normal(block(alpha,1,1,J,T-1),sigma_alpha);
>
> should work.
>
> For different sigma_alphas you'll need to loop over J:
>
> for (j in 1:J) {
>   block(alpha,j,2,1,T-1) ~ normal(block(alpha,j,1,1,T-1),sigma_alpha);
> }
>
>
> >
> > Best
> > Christopher
> >
> > On Tue, Sep 30, 2014 at 5:25 PM, Marcus Brubaker <marcus.a...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > >
> > >
> > > On Tue, Sep 30, 2014 at 10:37 AM, Christopher Gandrud <christoph...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > >
> > > > Hi everyone
> > > >
> > > > I have a hierarchical IRT model that runs ok. There are just two spots where I have made horrible hacks to accomplish my goals. I'm wondering if anyone could help me clean these up.
> > > >
> > > > The full model code is below, but I'll just highlight the hacks first.
> > > >
> > > > Hack 1: I am measuring ability (what I call transparency and denote alpha) for J countries for T years. For all years T > 1, the alpha Tn - 1 forms the prior for year T. I want to recenter the ability for T = 1 by subtracting the mean ability and dividing by the standard deviation.
> > > >
> > > > I do this in the transformed parameters block:
> > > > transformed parameters {
> > > >         // recenters transparency for t = 1
> > > >         vector[J] recentered_alpha1;
> > > >         real mean_alpha1;
> > > >         real<lower=0> sd_alpha1;
> > > >
> > > >         mean_alpha1 <- mean(alpha1);
> > > >         sd_alpha1 <- sd(alpha1);
> > > >         for (j in 1:J)
> > > >             recentered_alpha1[j] <- ( alpha1[j] - mean_alpha1 ) / sd_alpha1;
> > > > }
> > > >
> > > > The problem is that I want to put recentered_alpha[j] into an alpha matrix at alpha[j,1]. I can't seem to do this in the transformed parameters block so do the horrible hack:
> > > > alpha[j,1] ~ normal(recentered_alpha1[j], 0.001);
> > > >
> > > > in the model block.
> > >
> > >
> > > What you want to do is declare alpha as a transformed parameter, and create a variable alpha_raw which is a matrix[J,T-1].  E.G.,
> > >
> > > parameters {
> > >   ...
> > >   matrix[J,T-1] alpha_raw;
> > >   ...
> > > }
> > >
> > > transformed parameters {
> > >   matrix[J,T] alpha;
> > >
> > >   ...
> > >
> > >   for (j in 1:J) {
> > >     alpha[j,1] <- recentered_alpha1[j];
> > >     for (t in 2:T) {
> > >         alpha[j,t] <- alpha_raw[j,t-1];
> > >     }
> > >   }
> > > }
> > >
> > >
> > > >
> > > > Hack 2: I would like to have a half-Cauchy prior for each country but I am having trouble vectorising this. I think STAN doesn't support truncation with Cauchy distributions and I can't seem to set the lower bound in all items of a J length list. My solution so far basically kills a lot of the benefits of vectorisation:
> > > > for (j in 1:J) {
> > > >             alpha[j,1] ~ normal(recentered_alpha1[j], 0.001);
> > > >             sigma_alpha ~ cauchy(0,0.25);
> > > >             for (t in 2:T)
> > > >                 alpha[j,t] ~ normal(alpha[j,t-1], sigma_alpha);
> > > > }
> > > >
> > > > Any help would be much appreciated!
> > >
> > > Not sure I really follow what you're asking here, but you almost certainly don't want that sigma_alpha line inside the loop.  Doing so multiplies that prior into the posterior J times!
> > >
> > > Also, you should be able to vectorize the normal distributions if you are willing to sacrifice a little readibility for (potentially) a lot of speed.  See the block() command which extracts a submatrix and that entire double loop can collapse down into a single line.
> > >
> > > Cheers,
> > > Marcus
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > > >
> > > > The full model:
> > > >     data {
> > > >         int<lower=1> J;                // number of countries
> > > >         int<lower=1> T;                // number of years
> > > >         int<lower=1> K;                // number of items
> > > >         int<lower=1> N;                // number of observations
> > > >         int<lower=1> jj[N];            // country for observation n
> > > >         int<lower=1> tt[N];            // time for observation n
> > > >         int<lower=1,upper=K> kk[N];    // item for observation n
> > > >         int<lower=0,upper=1> y[N];     // response for observation n
> > > >     }
> > > >
> > > >     parameters {
> > > >         real delta;                    // mean transparency
> > > >         vector[J] alpha1;              // initial alpha for t = 1 before recentering
> > > >         matrix[J,T] alpha;             // transparency for j,t - mean
> > > >         vector[K] beta;                // difficulty of item k
> > > >         vector[K] log_gamma;           // discrimination of k
> > > >
> > > >         // all scale parameters have an implicit half Cauchy prior
> > > >         real<lower=0> sigma_alpha;     // scale of abilities
> > > >         real<lower=0> sigma_beta;      // scale of difficulties
> > > >         real<lower=0> sigma_gamma;     // scale of log discrimination
> > > >     }
> > > >
> > > >     transformed parameters {
> > > >         // recenters transparency for t = 1
> > > >         vector[J] recentered_alpha1;
> > > >         real mean_alpha1;
> > > >         real<lower=0> sd_alpha1;
> > > >
> > > >         mean_alpha1 <- mean(alpha1);
> > > >         sd_alpha1 <- sd(alpha1);
> > > >         for (j in 1:J)
> > > >             recentered_alpha1[j] <- ( alpha1[j] - mean_alpha1 ) / sd_alpha1;
> > > >     }
> > > >
> > > >     model {
> > > >         alpha1 ~ normal(0,100);                                 // diffuse prior
> > > >         for (j in 1:J) {
> > > >             alpha[j,1] ~ normal(recentered_alpha1[j], 0.001);   // horrible hack
> > > >             sigma_alpha ~ cauchy(0,0.25);
> > > >             for (t in 2:T)
> > > >                 alpha[j,t] ~ normal(alpha[j,t-1], sigma_alpha);
> > > >         }
> > > >         beta ~ normal(0,sigma_beta);
> > > >         log_gamma ~ normal(0,sigma_gamma);
> > > >         delta ~ cauchy(0,0.25);
> > > >         sigma_beta ~ cauchy(0,0.25);
> > > >         sigma_gamma ~ cauchy(0,0.25);
> > > >         for (n in 1:N)
> > > >             y[n] ~ bernoulli_logit(
> > > >                     exp(log_gamma[kk[n]])
> > > >                     * (alpha[jj[n],tt[n]] - beta[kk[n]] + delta) );
> > > > }
> > > >
