Well, I made some progress, but then got bogged down again. I managed to (I think) get a SUR equivalent to https://github.com/stan-dev/example-models/blob/master/ARM/Ch.17/17.1_radon_vary_inter_slope.stan to work. This is reproduced at the bottom of the page. However, I am trying to (as in https://github.com/stan-dev/example-models/blob/master/ARM/Ch.17/17.1_radon_correlation.stan) to model the correlation between the intercepts and slopes of the J_agencies. I am sure I am misunderstanding what the manual Chapter 4 says about orders of vectors and matrices, but really can't figure it out.

Working from the radon_correlation.stan example I think I should be writing something like the following. The [3] is my attempt to hard-code the three dependent variables in my analysis.

data {
                    int<lower=1> N_obs;

                    int<lower=1> J_agencies;
                    vector[3] y[N_obs];
                    int<lower=2006,upper=2010> year [N_obs];

                    int <lower=0> agency [N_obs];

                    }

                  parameters {
                    vector <lower=-1, upper=1> [3] rho_sur;

                    vector <lower=0, upper=10> [3] sigma_sur;
                    vector <lower=0, upper=10> [3] sigma_a;
                    vector <lower=0, upper=10> [3] sigma_b;
                    vector[3] a [J_agencies];
                    vector[3] b [J_agencies];
                    vector [3] mu_a;
                    vector [3] mu_b;
                    vector <lower=-1, upper=1> [3] rho;
                    vector [2] B_temp [3];

                                                   }

                 transformed parameters{
                    cov_matrix[3] Sigma_sur;
                    Sigma_sur[1,1] <- square(sigma_sur[1]);
                        Sigma_sur[2,2] <- square(sigma_sur[2]);
                    Sigma_sur[3,3] <- square(sigma_sur[3]);
                    Sigma_sur[1,2] <- rho_sur[1]*sigma_sur[1]*sigma_sur[2];
                    Sigma_sur[1,3] <- rho_sur[2]*sigma_sur[1]*sigma_sur[3];
                    Sigma_sur[2,3] <- rho_sur[3]*sigma_sur[2]*sigma_sur[3];
                    Sigma_sur[2,1] <- Sigma_sur[1,2];
                    Sigma_sur[3,1] <- Sigma_sur[1,3];
                    Sigma_sur[3,2] <- Sigma_sur[2,3];
                                                        }

                 model {
