Thank you for your suggestion Herra,

but I've already tried a model version with two separate a_ind parameters some time ago (and now again). It didn't help. Some chains are stuck on one mixture part, other chains are stuck on the other.
But I was thinking that it shouldn't matter if there is one a_ind parameter or two in this model. Since there are only two options in the model - either all subjects are using Delta rule (first mixture) or the Decay rule (second mixture). And therefore it shouldn't lead to any interaction between a_ind information from those two mixture parts. Right?

I've also tried to remove group-level hierarchical distributions for a_ind, w_ind, A_ind and c_ind. This also didn't help.

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Sun Oct 26 19:32:54 2014
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
