On Monday, October 27, 2014 11:31:16 AM UTC-4, Xulong Wang wrote:
> I am building an ordered categorical model with a random effect. The model can be written as: f(y) ~ x * beta + u, where u ~ (0, sigma * K), and K as a covariance matrix is known. The model as attached below is running fine but very slow. It takes hours for a 2000 iteration run. Could you please give me your suggestions to speed things up?

Yes, see edits to your model below. Basically, it comes down to the rule to not use any of the variants of multi_normal() unless the left-hand side is data.

> Secondly, in a separate model with an additional predictor, is it possible to reuse the estimation of the random effect u? I have to include thousands of genetic variants, means there will be thousands of run. So, speed and efficiency is important to me.

If I understand you correctly, you cannot do so in a theoretically satisfying way. This is similar to the discussion of the cut() function in the BUGS family of software.

> //--- The model
> data {
>   int<lower=1> N;  // Sample number
>   int<lower=1> K;  // Response categories
>   matrix[N,N] KS;  // Covariate matrix
>   int<lower=1,upper=K> Ad[N];  // Response
>   int<lower=1,upper=2> Sex[N];  //  M:F
> }
>
> transformed data {
>   matrix[N,N] CK;  // Cholesky K

>   # vector[N] mu;  // 0 (not needed anymore)

>
>   vector[N] sexF;  // Relative to M
>
>   CK <- cholesky_decompose(KS);
>
>   for (i in 1:N) {

>     # mu[i] <- 0; // not needed anymore

>
>     sexF[i]  <- Sex[i]  == 2;
>   }
> }
>
> parameters {

>   #vector[N] u;  // random effect
      vector[N] z; // primative of random effect

>   real<lower=0> sigmaU;
>
>   real pSex;  // Female
>
>   positive_ordered[K-1] cut;  // cut points
> }

> transformed data {
      vector[N] u;
      u <- sigmaU * (CK * z);
    }

> model {
>   vector[N] yhat;
>
>   pSex ~ normal(0, 5);
>   sigmaU ~ normal(0, 5);
>

>   #u ~ multi_normal_cholesky(mu, sigmaU * CK);
      z <- normal(0,1); // implies u ~ multi_normal()

>
>   yhat <- u + sexF * pSex;
>
>   for (n in 1:N)
>     Ad[n] ~ ordered_logistic(yhat[n], cut);
> }
>

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Mon Oct 27 17:33:28 2014
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Mon, 27 Oct 2014 17:33:28 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Re: Hierarchies in a seemingly unrelated regression?
Message-ID: <_W7hlFMnoDc.ybp35iv_ttwJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <_W7hlFMnoDc.Ygtu5Rx5QLAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <_W7hlFMnoDc.JUsQTVC5PdsJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <_W7hlFMnoDc.Ygtu5Rx5QLAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/_W7hlFMnoDc/m/ybp35iv_ttwJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Yes that will work as far as the Cholesky's concerned, but
it doesn't solve the major problem, which is that there's
no guarantee with this construction that Sigma is positive
definite.  It also won't improve efficiency if there's only
a single sampling statement (rather than a loop of them).

So what I'm suggesting is use a cholesky_factor_corr as a parameter,
provide an lkj_corr prior on that, have the same sigma[] array
with whatever priors you want, then use the quad form to put
it all together.  There's an example in the 2.5 manual in the
regression chapter in a section on multivariate priors.

- Bob

> On Oct 27, 2014, at 6:46 AM, Lindsay <lindsay...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Thanks, Bob,
>
> I've read the Stan manual on Cholesky decomposition. Would I be right in thinking that in Stan this would be as simple as re-writing the transformed parameters block as:
>
>  transformed parameters{
>          cov_matrix[3] Sigma;
>          matrix[3, 3] L;
>          Sigma[1,1] <- square(sigma[1]);
>          Sigma[2,2] <- square(sigma[2]);
>          Sigma[3,3] <- square(sigma[3]);
>          Sigma[1,2] <- rho[1]*sigma[1]*sigma[2];
>          Sigma[1,3] <- rho[2]*sigma[1]*sigma[3];
>          Sigma[2,3] <- rho[3]*sigma[2]*sigma[3];
>          Sigma[2,1] <- Sigma[1,2];
>          Sigma[3,1] <- Sigma[1,3];
>          Sigma[3,2] <- Sigma[2,3];
>          L <- cholesky_decompose(Sigma);
>                             }
>
> ...and rewriting the last line as:
>
> y ~ multi_normal_cholesky(y_hat, L);
>
> ?
