MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Your first approach is correct — you want to do a proper rejection sampling.

As always, if you’re not sure go ahead and simulate and compare the two
approaches.

On Oct 28, 2014, at 7:16 PM, Cody Ross <ctr...@ucdavis.edu> wrote:

>
> Hi all,
>
> I would like to generate a random negative binomial sample at each MCMC iteration, which is easy enough with neg_binomial_rng. The issue is that in my model I run:
> increment_log_prob((neg_binomial_log(Exposure[n],(Mu[1,n]*B[1]),B[1])-neg_binomial_cdf_log(MaxExposure, (Mu[1,n]*B[1]),B[1])));
> to implement a truncated density. Anything greater than MaxExposure is a priori impossible.
>
> So when I generate a random number, I want it to be from the truncated density. The T[,] operation doesn't work in the generated quantities block, so I wrote a little hack to resample anything that exceeds MaxExposure:
> vector[N] scrapExposure;
> vector[N*2] scrap;
> real Nscrap;
> int Ticker;
>
> ########################################################################### RNG
>   for( n in 1:N){
>       Ticker <- 1;
>       while (Ticker == 1) {
>         Nscrap <-neg_binomial_rng((exp(Theta[1])*B[1]),B[1]);
>         if(Nscrap<=MaxExposure){
>         Ticker<-0;}
>         else{
>         Ticker<-1;}}
>
>        scrapExposure[n]<-Nscrap;
>        }
>
> But I'm not sure if it should instead be something like this:
> vector[N] scrapExposure;
> vector[N*2] scrap;
> real Nscrap;
> int Ticker;
>
> ########################################################################### RNG
>   for( n in 1:N){
>         Nscrap <-neg_binomial_rng((exp(Theta[1])*B[1]),B[1]);
>         if(Nscrap<=MaxExposure){
>         scrapExposure[n]<-Nscrap;}
>         else{
>        scrapExposure[n]<-MaxExposure;}
> }
>
> where anything over the truncation point gets rounded down to the maximum.
>
> I think the first version is right but any feedback would be appreciated. Especially if there is already some code on how to do this.
>
>
> On a more fun note, I had a dream the other night where a private jet, a fighter jet escort, and a FedEx truck flew over my city... followed by tons of SWAT vehicles on the ground. Luckily, I was able to get to where they were all going---an unveiling of the next generation Google Glass. I pushed my way to the front, and saw the Google exec finish a demo on email using GoogleGlass, only to see Andrew Gelman take the glasses and say "If you think email on your glasses is cool, I can fit a mixed effects model on peoples shoes color predicting political affiliation, and run it in Stan right on my glasses" He then proceeded to tap the air in front of his glasses while looking at the crowd, to mark indicator variables that GoogleStan could automatically compile into a model. Before he finished he remarked it was stupid to fit a model of shoe color and political affiliation, and then left. The future looks bright!
>

> --
